{
  "packet_id": "a34562a861892e4b",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (decide whether to invest, based on evidence of demand, willingness to pay and value capture) from the suggested solution, and lists alternatives including maintaining the portfolio. It notes that reach to individual buyers is unknown. It does not reach 3. It never addresses the premise in 'beyond its existing professional offering', even though the dossier source simo-beanscene-prima-domestic shows Victoria Arduino already sells a domestic flagship, the Eagle One Prima. Alternative 3 also muddles segments by listing coffee shops and hotels, which are existing professional customers, as a 'different' segment. The €20,000 budget and 12-week timeline are constraints or assumptions, not observed facts."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "Scored on the best theory, T2 (A7). T2 says who changes behaviour (busy home users) and why (convenience and consistency), and gives a channel and pricing approach. It does not trace how Simonelli specifically captures value against established consumer-appliance competitors. 'Repeat purchases and loyalty' for durable machines is asserted, not explained. T1 (A6) is internally incoherent: it says consumers substitute home use for the café because the café offers social interaction they cannot replicate at home."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "T1 (social status and experience) and T2 (functional convenience) are genuine rival explanations of home-barista demand, with somewhat different positioning and channels: specialty or direct-to-consumer versus convenience retail. Both stay inside the same home-barista customer and product frame. No rival addresses small office, travel or professional-line extension, although A1 named these alternatives, so 3 is not met."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable: comparative purchase intent, survey rankings of purchase factors, and willingness-to-pay comparisons. Some would trouble the theory if they failed, such as social experience not ranking as a top factor for T1. However, the assumptions are vague, for example 'market large enough to support premium pricing' with no threshold. The predictions are framed mainly as mirror-image comparisons, not as conditions whose failure would sink the investment case."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A8"],
      "reason": "No source assessment events occurred before the run stopped at A8. Separately, T1 (A6) misreads simo-coffeegeek-pandemic-2021, which is flagged below."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A2", "A3", "A4", "A5", "A8"],
      "reason": "The searches in A2–A5 are generic market and company searches, not searches directed against a favoured theory. The investigation stopped (A8) before any challenge stage."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A8"],
      "reason": "No test was proposed or declined before the run stopped at A8."
    },
