      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A8"],
      "reason": "Revision is applicable and an update was scheduled, but update_delivered is false and the run stopped at A8."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "not_reached",
      "score": null,
      "event_ids": ["A8"],
      "reason": "A8 states that the investigation stopped before a recommendation because of repeated errors."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "incomplete",
      "event_ids": ["A8"],
      "reason": "The episode ended without a completed recommendation and with no evidence assessment, test, or revision."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A3"],
      "reason": "simo-coffeegeek-pandemic-2021 says the café offers social interaction that being a home barista 'can never offer'. That points away from home equipment as a social substitute. T1 nonetheless builds a theory that home-barista demand is driven by social interaction and status, and its mechanism contradicts itself. The source does not support the inference."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "Hypotheses are stated as facts. Both theories open with declarative claims ('Home barista demand is driven by...') that no source in the dossier establishes."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "Rejected. The episode is incomplete: it stopped at A8 with no recommendation, assessment, test, or revision. It also contains an unsupported inference in T1 (A6), which misreads the CoffeeGeek source."
}

{
  "packet_id": "0677afed88510a8c",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: home barista, travel, small-office, a different route to market, and the status quo. It does not reach rung 3 because it misses a premise the dossier exposes. simo-dcn-oscar-mood-2021 shows Simonelli already sells the Oscar line for homes and prosumers, so the issue is differentiating an existing consumer presence rather than going 'beyond' professional equipment. A1 never notes this."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 names a benefit: professional-quality coffee at home. Its behaviour driver mixes 'convenience' with 'the social experience of coffee preparation'. The cited forum post says home barista use can never offer social interaction, so this driver conflicts with the dossier. Value capture is generic ('retail channels and online platforms'). It is not traced to what Simonelli would earn versus the Oscar line or competitors."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T2 is a near-rewording of T1. Its who-benefits, behaviour, value-capture, assumption and prediction text is almost identical, differing only in 'home barista machine' versus 'home coffee machine'. Neither theory considers hobby or skill learning, status, small-office use, or a no-demand rival."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "Assumptions are stated, but the predictions are vague or odd. The survey prediction relies on 'a significant percentage' without a threshold. The prediction that a consumer machine will have higher average revenue per unit than professional machines is implausible and poorly linked to the theory. The predictions are not sharp enough that failure would clearly endanger the theory."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 0,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14", "A15", "A16", "A17"],
      "reason": "The evidence is systematically misread. A11 classes a forum comment as an 'independent_study' and records it as supporting T1, although the post argues against home barista culture. A14 and A15 record De'Longhi's consumer-coffee decline as supporting T1 and T2. A16 records the PDG slowdown as support. A12 and A17 treat a De'Longhi earnings statement about La Marzocco and Eversys as support for Simonelli consumer demand. Each 'does not show' note is boilerplate. None addresses date, place, or fit with the customer group."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14", "A15", "A16"],
      "reason": "A13 does search for evidence against T1 and finds genuinely contrary sources: the slowdown and decline reports. The results are then recorded as support (A14–A16) rather than reported fairly as challenges. The search had value, but the reporting is not honest."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "The survey compares professional-grade and simplified machines. That loosely separates the two rival theories, but the rivals are near-duplicates. It relies on stated willingness to pay. The method text and decision rule are truncated. The €20 threshold is justified by 'a pilot test showing a €15 difference', an observation that appears nowhere in the dossier. Because the observation is invented, this dimension is capped at 1."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A20", "A21"],
      "reason": "A20 correctly notes the scope limits of SYN-SIMO-UPDATE. It then declares the update has no effect on T1. It ignores that the result (72 versus 32 reservations) bears directly on a learning or skill mechanism and on the value proposition, and it does not mark the result as simulated in its reasoning. A21 then silently adds 'a structured learning experience' to the value proposition, untraced to the update. The response is neither proportionate nor consistent."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A19", "A21"],
      "reason": "A21 commits to a next action and states reversal conditions. However, 'T1: likely' rests on misread evidence: the contrary decline and slowdown sources were recorded as support. The second reversal condition, a sustained decline in demand, is already suggested by the cited sources, yet the recommendation stands. The position does not follow from the evidence."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "misleading_quote",
      "event_ids": ["A11"],
      "reason": "The coffeegeek quote is clipped to the 'third place' sentence and recorded as support for home barista demand. The full excerpt says home barista use can never offer social interaction, which argues against T1."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A14", "A15", "A16"],
      "reason": "Sources reporting a consumer-coffee decline and an equipment sales slowdown are recorded as supporting growth-based theories T1 and T2."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A17"],
      "reason": "A De'Longhi company assertion about La Marzocco and Eversys entering the prosumer market is treated as evidence of demand for a Simonelli consumer proposition."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A19", "A21"],
      "reason": "T1 rests on a 'social experience' driver that contradicts the cited source. It is rated 'likely' without supporting evidence."
    },
    {
      "kind": "invented_observation",
      "event_ids": ["A18"],
      "reason": "A 'pilot test showing a €15 difference' is cited to justify the decision threshold. No such pilot exists in the packet."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A20", "A21"],
      "reason": "A20 declares the simulated update irrelevant to T1, yet A21 adopts a learning element in the value proposition without tracing it to the update or noting that the result is simulated."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A16"],
      "reason": "Source classes are mislabelled. A forum comment and a trade-press opinion piece are labelled 'independent_study', although both are verified observations or commentary rather than studies."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete but contains critical errors. These include a clipped quote that reverses the source's meaning (A11), contrary evidence recorded as support (A14–A16), and a company marketing claim treated as demand evidence (A12, A17). The experiment's threshold rests on an invented pilot observation (A18). The update is handled inconsistently (A20–A21), and the two theories are near-duplicates. Any one of these alone would reject the trajectory."
}

{
  "packet_id": "743f67d4ef377738",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 frames the objective as whether the evidence justifies investment, which separates it from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: no expansion, adjacent expansion, and licensing to or partnering with consumer brands. It also states constraints, including the unknown current reach to individual buyers. It does not correct a false premise or surface a non-obvious constraint. For example, it misses that the consumer-operated formats are already professional channels. It also misnames the company as 'Electron’x'."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "The chains are weak. T1 (A6) is internally confused: homeowners 'switch from doing laundry at home to using a professional machine', yet a home machine is still laundry at home. Its claim that this 'saves time and money' has no support. Its 20–30% premium is an assumption, not a traced capture mechanism. T2 (A7) names property managers and high-volume need. However, that is essentially the existing professional consumer-operated business, not a consumer proposition. Neither theory goes beyond naming a benefit and a pricing assertion."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "T1 (homeowners, premium, retail) and T2 (property managers, high capacity, wholesale/lease) differ in customer and channel, so they are genuine rivals. However, both open with the same 'Homeowners and small businesses will adopt' premise. T2 also blurs into the existing professional segment rather than a distinct consumer proposition, which limits how distinct they really are."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A6", "A7"],
      "reason": "Both theories state observable predictions, such as '≥15% of surveyed homeowners will express intent' at a 25% premium, and '≥20% of property managers'. The thresholds are arbitrary, and they rely on stated intent rather than behaviour. The secondary predictions (retail vs online, lease vs purchase) would not put either theory in trouble if they failed."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A8", "A9", "A11", "A13", "A5", "A10"],
      "reason": "A8 notes what the residential-segment quote does not show. However, A13 and A15 then restate it as 'Public evidence shows residential customers are demanding commercial‑quality home appliances'. The excerpt's 'our machines' is a marketing assertion, and the agent never asks whose it is or whether it applies to this manufacturer. A11 cites the same home-market quote as support for the multi-family theory T2, which it does not support. Sources that bear directly on T1 were retrieved but never assessed: the Speed Queen small-share observation (A5) and the Which? no-premium finding (A10). There is no treatment of date, place, or fit to the customer group."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "A10 ran one search against T1 and listed results, but never reported what they showed or how they bear on T1. There was no challenge to T2. The potentially contrary Speed Queen evidence (A5) was ignored."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "Several flaws mean the test cannot separate the theories.\n- It recruits only homeowners and small business owners, so T2's customers (property managers) are absent. A home-framing win is then expected under either theory.\n- It describes a 'consumer‑grade machine' versus a 'high‑capacity machine' while claiming 'identical product specifications'.\n- The measure is inconsistent: a 7-point scale versus '1–10'.\n- It relies on stated intent only.\n- The decision rule turns a relative messaging gap below 1.5 points into 'focus on the professional business'. A relative gap says nothing about absolute consumer demand."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "A14 claims SYN-EPRO-UPDATE 'does not address T1'. Yet the simulated result tests durability and repairability (long-life, professional-derived) framing against performance and price on paid refundable reservations: 72 vs 32 of 400. That bears directly on T1's core assumption that long-life positioning drives purchase. A proportionate response would treat it as what-if support for durability framing, within its stated limits. It would also note implications for the test design. Dismissing it as irrelevant is unsound."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A15"],
      "reason": "A15 gives a position, a next action and reversal conditions. However, it rests on a marketing claim restated as fact and ignores the delivered update. Its reversal conditions depend on the non-discriminating decision rule from A12. It is also inconsistent: it 'favours T1' and specifies a T1 value proposition while recommending against investment."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A15"],
      "reason": "A one-line marketing assertion ('our machines represent a compelling fit') is presented as 'Public evidence shows residential customers are demanding...'. This treats a marketing claim as evidence of demand."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A11"],
      "reason": "A quote about residential customers wanting commercial-quality home products is cited as support for the multi-family, high-capacity theory T2. The quote does not bear on that theory."
    },
    {
      "kind": "non_discriminating_test",
      "event_ids": ["A12"],
      "reason": "The test samples only homeowners and small business owners, so T2's target customers are absent. Its product descriptions are contradictory, its scale is inconsistent, and its decision rule maps a relative messaging gap to an absolute investment decision. It cannot separate T1 from T2."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A14"],
      "reason": "A simulated result directly relevant to T1's durability and long-life mechanism (72 vs 32 reservations) is dismissed as not addressing T1. The update is ignored rather than proportionately weighed."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A1", "A13", "A15"],
      "reason": "The company is repeatedly misnamed ('Electron’x', 'Electron‑x'). Contrary sources retrieved in A5 and A10 are never assessed."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "Two critical errors each disqualify the trajectory on their own: the non-discriminating test (A12) and the unsound dismissal of the relevant update (A14). The trajectory also treats a marketing claim as evidence of demand (A13, A15) and makes an unsupported evidence mapping (A11)."
}

{
  "packet_id": "85a13c13187ad36b",
  "dimensions": {
    "problem_framing": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "A1 separates the objective (whether evidence justifies investment) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives, including no expansion, a route-to-market change and a different professional customer group. It does not flag the non-obvious premise that consumer-operated shared laundry is already part of the existing professional offering (epro-consumer-operated, epro-selfservice-formats), so it is not 'beyond' it. It also misses the channel-absence constraint implied by epro-reviewed-speedqueen-small. The €20,000 and 12-week constraints do not appear in the case dossier and are unexplained."
    },
    "causal_mechanism": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A5), names a benefit (lower operational cost, uptime) but confuses who buys. Residents and students in multi-family buildings do not usually purchase the shared machines; building operators do. 'Residents ... benefit from reduced labor costs' is incoherent as a behaviour-change chain. Value capture through premium machines and service contracts is asserted, not traced from a coherent buyer decision."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"],
      "reason": "T1 and T2 share the same product, customer, channel and settings, and even an identical prediction (higher adoption in multi-family than single-family). They differ only in the stated motive, cost versus convenience. They are near-rewordings of one adoption idea. Neither is a rival such as household premium durability, retail/wholesale compact machines (epro-gen7000-retail-wholesale) or no viable consumer demand."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A5", "A6"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable, for example survey choices and willingness to pay with a downtime warranty. However, they are stated-preference survey outcomes, so T1 would not be in serious trouble if they failed. The 10% thresholds are unexplained assumptions. The shared adoption prediction does not discriminate between the theories."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A7", "A8", "A13"],
      "reason": "A7 labels epro-alliance-residential-segment an 'independent_study'. It is a verified observation of another company's statement about 'our machines' and a select residential home segment. A7 then applies it to self-service shared spaces, which the quote does not cover. A8 cites a company claim describing capabilities as support for T2. The 'does not show' limits are partly sensible, but there is no attention to source type, place, fit with the customer group, or the ignored sources (epro-reviewed-speedqueen-small, epro-uba-replace-working). A13 mischaracterises the evidence base as 'a single simulated result ... and a company claim'."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "A9 searches against T1 and lists 'epro-which-no-premium', a source absent from the dossier, as potentially contrary. It never reports or weighs what was found, and no later event engages it. This amounts to looking without reporting fairly."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "Comparing a cost frame with a convenience frame could in principle separate T1 from T2. The design has several defects. It samples residents and students, who are not the machine buyers. It mixes a 'percentage points' rule with average willingness to pay. The decision rule is truncated ('consider a'). It never tests the go/no-go question shared by both theories. Its decision relevance and validity are therefore weak."
    },
    "revision": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A13"],
      "reason": "A12 states that SYN-EPRO-UPDATE 'does not bear on T1'. Yet the durability and repairability framing, which aligns with T1's long-term reliability and uptime value, drew 32 reservations versus 72 for the performance-and-price framing. That bears at least on T1's premium-for-reliability assumption, even allowing that household buyers are a different population. A13 then lists as a change condition 'if the simulated result were to show a meaningful difference', when the result already showed one in the direction against T1. Retention may have been defensible on population-fit grounds, but the stated reasoning misreads the update."
    },
    "recommendation": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A13"],
      "reason": "A13 recommends 'Invest' while its own next action is a validating survey. This conflicts with the A1 constraint that any recommendation rest on observable demand. The support rests on a misapplied quote. One reversal condition is illogical: 'strong consumer demand ... we would reconsider the investment'. The recommendation also targets a format the company already offers professionally. It states a position without adequate support."
    }
  },
  "red_flags": [
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A7", "A11", "A13"],
      "reason": "A quote about a competitor's machines for a select residential home segment is used as support for premium self-service machines in shared buildings. That support then underpins an 'Invest' recommendation."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A8"],
      "reason": "A company capability claim (epro-consumer-operated) is treated as support for a demand theory, which amounts to a marketing claim used as evidence of demand."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsupported_inference",
      "event_ids": ["A5"],
      "reason": "The mechanism has residents buying shared-building machines and benefiting from 'reduced labor costs', which is an incoherent buyer chain."
    },
    {
      "kind": "unsound_revision",
      "event_ids": ["A12", "A13"],
      "reason": "The update dismisses a relevant simulated result as irrelevant to T1, and then treats the already-observed large difference as hypothetical."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "The challenge search cites 'epro-which-no-premium', which does not exist in the dossier. Its content is never reported or weighed, so potential counter-evidence was ignored."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A10"],
      "reason": "The test samples non-buyers, mixes the units of the decision rule, and has a truncated decision rule."
    },
    {
      "kind": "other",
      "event_ids": ["A1"],
      "reason": "The budget and timeline constraints (€20,000, 12 weeks) do not appear in the case dossier and are not explained."
    }
  ],
  "suitable_for_training": false,
  "screen_reason": "The episode is complete, but it contains several critical errors. A source is misclassified and misapplied as support (A7). A company claim is used as evidence of demand (A8). The update misreads a relevant simulated result (A12, A13). Challenge evidence from an off-dossier source is ignored (A9). The recommendation to invest is unsupported (A13). Any one of these rejects the trajectory."
}

